-- title:
--   Idempotent-like operators from scalar actions separating indices
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, $V$ a $K$-vector space, and $\iota$ a finite type with decidable equality. Given a family $W : \iota \to$ (submodules of $V$), a set $\mathcal{T}$ of $K$-linear endomorphisms of $V$, and a function $c$ assigning to each endomorphism $T$ and each index $i$ a scalar $c\,T\,i \in K$, assume: (i) every $T \in \mathcal{T}$ acts on each $W i$ by the scalar $c\,T\,i$, that is $T v = (c\,T\,i) \cdot v$ for all $v \in W i$ and all $i$; and (ii) the scalars separate indices: for all $i \neq j$ there is $T \in \mathcal{T}$ with $c\,T\,i \neq c\,T\,j$. Then for each index $i$ there exists an element $e$ of the $K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ K\ \mathcal{T}$ of $\mathrm{End}_K(V)$ generated by $\mathcal{T}$ such that $e v = v$ for every $v \in W i$, and $e v = 0$ for every index $j \neq i$ and every $v \in W j$. No independence, spanning or finite-dimensionality assumption on the subspaces $W j$ is made, and $e$ is only asserted to be a projector in this relative sense, not an idempotent of $\mathrm{End}_K(V)$.
--
--   This is the Lagrange-interpolation construction of the projectors cut out inside a commutative algebra of operators by eigenvalue systems that separate a finite list of subspaces; in the theory of modular forms it produces, inside the Hecke algebra, the element acting as the identity on one eigenspace and as zero on the others. It is used in the proof that a space of cusp forms for $\Gamma_H$ has a one-element basis with one-dimensional eigenspace, via [`CuspForm.nonempty_basis_fin_one_gammaH_and_finrank_eigenspace_eq_one`](thm.html#CuspForm.nonempty_basis_fin_one_gammaH_and_finrank_eigenspace_eq_one).
